Let $1\le\ell<n$. If $\rho$ is a finite measure with $\rho\ll\sigma$, then the image $g_\#\rho$ of $\rho$ under every full-row-rank $g\in\mathbb R^{\ell\times d}$ has a Lebesgue density. For $\delta>0$, put $$p_{\rho,\delta}(g,y)
   =(2\delta)^{-\ell}\int
       \mathbf 1_{\{\left\lVert gu-y\right\rVert_\infty\le\delta\}}\,d\rho(u).$$ A jointly Borel density version is $$\label{eq:r-density-version}
 p_\rho(g,y)=\lim_{j\to\infty}p_{\rho,1/j}(g,y)$$ where the limit exists and is finite, and zero otherwise.*


*Suppose now that $\nu$ is a probability measure with $\nu\le H\sigma$, $H<\infty$, and $G\sim\gamma_\ell$ is independent of $S\sim\nu$. Then $\log p_\nu(G,GS)$ is integrable. In addition, $$\label{eq:r-first-density-moment}
 \sup_{\delta>0}\mathbb Ep_{\nu,\delta}(G,GS)
 \le(2\pi)^{-\ell/2}
       \iint\left\lVert u-s\right\rVert^{-\ell}\,d\nu(u)d\nu(s)<\infty,$$ and the same upper bound holds for $\mathbb Ep_\nu(G,GS)$.*

*Finally, let a countable $W$ with $H(W)<\infty$ have any joint law with $(S,G)$ that leaves the latter’s product marginal unchanged. For each positive-probability $w$, put $\nu_w=P_{S\mid W=w}$. The actual conditional law of $GS$ given $(G,W)$ has a Lebesgue density. The logarithm of that density and $\log p_{\nu_W}(G,GS)$ are both integrable under the actual law.

In the two experiments just defined, all quantities below are finite, and $$\begin{aligned}
 J_0(W)-J_1(W)
 &=h_0(Y\mid G,W)-h_1(Y\mid G,W)-I_1(S;G\mid W)
 \label{eq:r-information-identity}\\
 &\le h_0(Y\mid G,W)+\mathbb E_1\log p_{\nu_W}(G,Y).
 \label{eq:r-information-density-bound}
\end{aligned}$$ If $E=E(S,W)$ is countable and $H(E\mid W)<\infty$, and the same deterministic refinement $W'=(W,E)$ is used in both laws, then $$\label{eq:r-refinement-charge}
 J_0(W)-J_1(W)
 \le J_0(W')-J_1(W')+H(E\mid W).$$

Let $n=d-1$, let $m,r,q\ge1$ be integers, and put $\ell=m+r$. Suppose $$\label{eq:r-mixed-dimensions}
 \ell<n,\qquad \ell-m-(q-1)\ge1.$$ Let $\rho$ be a finite nonnegative Borel measure on $S^{d-1}$. Suppose that $B,D>0$ and, for every $z\in\mathbb R^d$ and $t>0$, $$\label{eq:r-mixed-growth}
 \rho(B(z,t))\le Bt^\ell,\qquad
 \rho(B(z,t))\le Dt^n.$$ Let $X\sim\gamma_m$ and $Z\sim\gamma_r$ be independent, and set $G=(X;Z)$. For $\delta>0$, define the cube average $$p_{\rho,\delta}(g,y)
   =(2\delta)^{-\ell}\int
       \mathbf 1_{\{\left\lVert gu-y\right\rVert_\infty\le\delta\}}\,d\rho(u).$$ There is an absolute $C$ such that, for every fixed $s\in S^{d-1}$ and every $\delta>0$, $$\label{eq:r-mixed-smoothed}
 \left[\mathbb E_X
       \left(\mathbb E_Z p_{\rho,\delta}((X;Z),(X;Z)s)\right)^q
 \right]^{1/q}
 \le e^{Cd}B\bigl(1+\log^+(D/B)\bigr).$$*


*If in addition $\rho\ll\sigma$, use the jointly Borel density $p_\rho$ in <a href="#eq:r-density-version" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:r-density-version">[eq:r-density-version]</a>. There is a measurable set $E_\rho\subset S^{d-1}$, with $\rho(E_\rho)=0$, such that for every $s\notin E_\rho$ the cube averages converge to $p_\rho(G,Gs)$ for $\gamma_\ell$-almost every $G$, and $$\label{eq:r-mixed-exact}
 \left[\mathbb E_X
       \left(\mathbb E_Zp_\rho((X;Z),(X;Z)s)\right)^q
 \right]^{1/q}
 \le e^{Cd}B\bigl(1+\log^+(D/B)\bigr).$$ When $\rho\ne0$, $E_\rho$ may also be chosen so that $0<p_\rho(G,Gs)<\infty$ for $\gamma_\ell$-almost every $G$ and every $s\notin E_\rho$.

Let $w_a>0$ and $\mu=\nu(\,\cdot\,\mid F_a)$. For independent $X\sim\gamma_k$, $B\sim\gamma_j$, and $G=(X;B)$, $$\label{rlevels:one-moment-bound}
 \left[\mathbb E_X
   \bigl(\mathbb E_B p_\mu(G,Gs)\bigr)^q\right]^{1/q}
 \le e^{Cd}\frac{a}{w_a}(1+d+\log K)$$ for $\mu$-almost every $s$.

For the preceding experiments and parameters, $$\label{rlevels:one-comparison-bound}
 \mathcal D(U)\le\widetilde{\mathcal D}(U)
 +C\left(d+\log(1+d+H(U))+\frac{H(U)}d\right).$$ The proposition applies to every finite $U$ with the specified joint law.

For the block above, with $1\le m\le h_0$ and $H(V)\le B$, $$\label{rhyb:block-cost}
 \overline J(V)-\overline J(U)
 \le Cm+\frac{m}{N_k}\left(C+\frac{B}{h_0}\right)+\frac{B}{q}.$$ When $B\le d^2$, the right side is at most $C'd$ for an absolute constant $C'$.

Let $D_j=(X,XS)$ consist of $j$ independent Gaussian measurements, where $0\le j<d$, and put $n=d-j$. Conditional on $D_j$, the signal is uniform on a sphere of radius $R$ in the affine solution space. Marginally, $$\label{rhyb:radius}
 R^2\stackrel{\mathrm{law}}=
 \frac{G_n}{G_n+G_j},
 \qquad
 \mathbb E R^{-2}=1+\frac{j}{n-2}\quad(n>2),$$ where $G_n,G_j$ are independent chi-squared variables and $G_0=0$.*

*If $j\le 3d/4$, the entropy of one further label given its row and $D_j$ lies between two absolute constants, and its conditional log density is absolutely integrable. Joint label entropies, and the conditional label entropies obtained by also conditioning on a finite state $V$, are finite whenever the total number of rows is at most $3d/4$. The associated conditional log densities are absolutely integrable. Removing $V$ can increase any such joint entropy by at most $H(V)$.*


*Let $\sigma_R$ be uniform probability on a sphere of radius $R>0$ in an $n$-dimensional affine space. A Euclidean ball of radius $a$ with arbitrary center has $\sigma_R$-mass at most $(2a/R)^{n-1}$ when $2a/R\le 1/2$. For $n\ge3$, $$\label{rhyb:reciprocal-sphere}
 \sup_{s\in\operatorname{supp}\sigma_R}
 \int\frac{\sigma_R(dt)}{\|t-s\|}
 \le \frac{C}{R}.$$

For $k_0\le k\le k_1$ and $0\le i\le m$, let $Z^{(i,k)}$ be a fresh matrix of $k-i$ rows and define $$G_i(k)=
 h\bigl((Y_{\le i},Z^{(i,k)}S)\mid X_{\le i},Z^{(i,k)},V\bigr)
 +I(S;X_{\le i}\mid V).$$ For $i<m$, put $x=X_{i+1}$, $Y=xS$, $L=L_i$, and $r=k-i-1$. With $F_r=(Z,ZS)$ denoting common fresh data of size $r$, $$\label{rhyb:hybrid-step}
 G_i(k)-G_{i+1}(k)
 =a_{r+1}^{(i)}
 -\bigl[h(Y\mid x,F_r,L)+I(S;x\mid F_r,L)\bigr].$$ The actual-row divergence costs are $$\begin{aligned}
 K_i&:=\mathbb E D_{\rm KL}
 \bigl(\mathcal L(X_{i+1}\mid S,L_i)\,\|\,\gamma\bigr)
 =I(X_{i+1};V\mid S,X_{\le i}),\label{rhyb:row-cost}\\
 \sum_{i=0}^{m-1}K_i
 &=I(X_1,\ldots,X_m;V\mid S)\le H(V)\le B.\label{rhyb:row-budget}
\end{aligned}$$ Finally, $$\label{rhyb:block-reduction}
 J_k(V)\le J_k(U)+G_0(k)-G_m(k).$$

In the preceding experiment, $A(S,L,F_r)\ge1/2$ almost surely and $\mathbb E A(S,L,F_r)<\infty$ for each fixed $d$. For almost every Gaussian row $x$, the projection of $\mu$ by $s\mapsto xs$ has a Lebesgue density $p_{\mu,x}$. With $q=\lfloor d/32\rfloor$, $$\begin{aligned}
 h(xS\mid x,L,F_r)+I(S;x\mid L,F_r)
 &\ge-\mathbb E\log p_{\mu,x}(xS),\label{rhyb:cross-entropy}\\
 \mathbb E\log\frac{p_{\mu,x}(xS)}{A(S,L,F_r)}
 &\le C+\frac{K_i}{q}.\label{rhyb:row-log-bound}
\end{aligned}$$ Both expectations use the actual row law. The logarithms in these displays are absolutely integrable.

Assume $1\le k<d-1$. For every bounded Borel function $H(C,s,t)$ on $\mathbb R^{k\times d}\times S^{d-1}\times S^{d-1}$, $$\begin{aligned}
 &\int \gamma_k(dC)\int\sigma(ds)\,p_C(Cs)
       \int\sigma_{C,Cs}(dt)\,H(C,s,t) \notag\\
 &\quad=(2\pi)^{-k/2}\int\sigma(ds)\int\sigma(dt)\,
       \|s-t\|^{-k}
       \int\gamma_{k,\perp\widehat{s-t}}(dC)\,H(C,s,t),
                                                        \label{rfib:two-point-id}
\end{aligned}$$ where $\widehat{s-t}=(s-t)/\|s-t\|$ off the diagonal. The diagonal has product measure zero, and its assigned value is immaterial. Both sides define finite Borel measures.

Let $\mu$ be a finite measure on $S^{d-1}$ of mass at most $2^k$ satisfying <a href="#rfib:tube" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="rfib:tube">[rfib:tube]</a> for every linear subspace of dimension $j\le\ell-1$. Define the finite measure on $n\times d$ matrices $$\lambda=\int\gamma_{n,\perp u}\,d\mu(u).$$ This is a Borel measure: a perpendicular matrix can be realized as $Z(I-uu^{\mathsf T})$ with $Z\sim\gamma_n$. Then every bounded Borel function $H$ on this matrix space satisfies $$\label{rfib:mixture-dual}
 \left|\int H\,d\lambda\right|
 \le e^{Kd}\|H\|_{L^{\ell/(\ell-1)}(\gamma_n)} .$$

Consider a fixed-seed Borel learner from Lemmas <a href="#lem:r-seed-reduction" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:r-seed-reduction">2</a> and <a href="#lem:r-borel-kernels" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:r-borel-kernels">25</a>. Suppose its accuracy satisfies $0<\epsilon\le1/10$, its persistent states have at most $2^M$ values, and its deterministic finite horizon is $T$. Write $L=\log(1/\epsilon)$. It admits an absorbing extension, including to the end of a longer final block, with at most $$N=(T+2)2^M$$ values at each layer. The terminal output is a kernel of the extended state alone. The initial extended state is independent of the joint signal and row sequence. At every deterministic block boundary, unused future rows are independent of the pair consisting of the signal and the entering extended state. The block transition is a Borel kernel of that state and the exact block data.*


*If its uniform-prior angular success probability is $p$, then $$\label{eq:r-common-output-capacity}
 p\le (T+1)2^M\epsilon^{d-1}.$$ For a sequence of such learners with $p\ge3/5$ and $M(d)=o(d^2)$, on the branch $T\le dL$, $$\label{eq:r-common-clock}
 dL=O(M+1),\qquad L=o(d),\qquad
 \log N=M\log2+\log(T+2)=o(d^2).$$ All these estimates are uniform over the allowed accuracies. For all sufficiently large $d$, even a learner given all its exact observations must have $T>d/4$ to achieve success at least $3/5$.

Let $S\sim\sigma$, let $V$ be a finite state jointly distributed with $S$, and let $G$ have $0\le k<d-1$ independent standard Gaussian rows, independently of $(S,V)$. Suppose a unit-vector output is drawn from a kernel of $V$ alone and has angular success probability at least $p$ at accuracy $\epsilon>0$. Let $R$ be the radius of the conditional sphere of $S$ given $(G,GS)$. If $$0<\rho\le1,\qquad 2\epsilon<\rho,\qquad
 \Pr\{R<\rho\}\le\eta<p,$$ then $$\label{eq:r-residual-terminal}
 I(S;V\mid G,GS)
 \ge(p-\eta)(d-k-1)\log\frac{\rho}{2\epsilon}-\log2.$$ For $\rho<1$, one available bad-radius bound is $$\label{eq:r-residual-markov}
 \Pr\{R<\rho\}\le\frac{k}{d(1-\rho^2)}.$$
